{{Economy of Balisca}}

The '''Baliscan sol''' ({{IPA-bl|sol}}; [[wikipedia:Currency sign|currency sign]]: '''S/''', [[wikipedia:ISO 4217|code]]: '''SOL'''; also abbreviated '''SL''') is the official currency of the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]]. It is subdivided into 100 centésimo. In line with Balisca's cooperative economic model, the sol is not a freely-traded international currency. The Confederal Banking Union maintains a policy of strict [[W:Capital controls|capital controls]] designed to insulate the domestic economy from global financial market speculation. This policy ensures the sol's value remains tied to Balisca's real productive economy rather than [[W:Speculation|speculative trading]].

Consequently, the Baliscan sol is one of the world's least-traded major currencies and is not held as a [[W:Reserve currency|reserve currency]] or portfolio asset by international traders. Its low volatility, a direct result of regulatory controls, makes it an unattractive target for the [[W:Foreign exchange market|foreign exchange]] (forex) market. [[W:International trade|International trade]] is primarily conducted through [[W:Bilateralism|bilateral agreements]] rather than the open market. The [[Confederal Banking Federation]] negotiates fixed exchange rates directly with the central or cooperative banks of its trading partners, particularly those in [[Abyala]] and [[Sudanese Kaftia]], to facilitate the exchange of goods and services while bypassing speculative currency markets.

